WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
what is so bad  is this crisis  is man-made  it is caused by deliberate political  decisions  by this administration by joe biden  and by cumberland  texas lawmakers are tearing into the  biden administration over the border  crisis as migrants continue to pour into  the u.s and the situation grows  increasingly dangerous for border patrol  agents joining me now to discuss retired  acting ice director and former border  patrol chief ron betello sir it is good  to see you this morning thank you for  being here we were just talking about in  the commercial break because i really  wanted to try and understand this a  little better uh the fact that you were  saying that at this point in the year we  should be seeing these numbers of  crossers trailing off until the end of  the year but you said that rulebook is  no longer here that is thrown out the  window can you explain  yes we are in  completely unprecedented territory you  know july went into the books at 212 000  plus  apprehensions encounters  at the southwest border july august  september traditionally had been lower  months of activity and so we're seeing  an unprecedented crush of humanity  coming to this border right now i  suspect that august is going to be in  similar territory it's going to be a  record uh july was a 21-year record  the department has never seen anything  as big as we're seeing right now and we  may be in record territory as it relates  to the the entire history of our u.s  mexico border it's really unbelievable  and it's not just migrants who are  coming across it's the drugs too take a  look at these numbers the weight of  fentanyl seized at the border and as you  can see here this is in pounds i mean  look at this this is up nearly double  from just a year ago and nearly triple  from two years ago you're talking more  than 8 500 pounds of fentanyl seized at  the border and this is not just a border  problem then this is a problem for every  community in the united states correct  correct because that those drugs don't  stay at the border in places like rgb or  in laredo or del rio those are being  trafficked all over the united states  and  you know  those gangs uh those drugs are being  sold in communities across the united  states and so when you have i was at the  border a couple of weeks ago when you  have 40 percent of the workforce doing  something else besides patrolling the  border that means you're going to get  more chaos you're going to get more drug  smuggling you're going to get more human  trafficking children are going to be  exploited people in the pipeline are  in danger and again that stuff doesn't  stay at the border it goes throughout  the united states and so when you have  this kind of flow and a distracted  border patrol who are trained to patrol  the border not care and comfort for kids  and families  that's that's the result of what you're  going to have and then i'm grateful for  the cbp officers who stop that load of  fentanyl because that stuff is dangerous  in very very small quantities and so the  more of that that they can do the better  off we are but if as as this flow  is unending we're going to have more  challenges at the border we're going to  have more challenges like that  throughout the homeland well yeah and  they're trying to do their jobs the  border patrol agents and then they're up  against being assaulted themselves uh we  have a number of examples on wednesday  five people were arrested after an agent  was assaulted by a smuggler and then we  have another example that happened in  the overnight hours on saturday this was  around 3 15 in the morning let's go  ahead and pull up this quote here from  u.s customs and border protection and  this reads the agent was attempting to  apprehend approximately 12 to 15  migrants a smuggler in mexico struck out  at the agent through a slot in the wall  hitting the agent with a large object  believed to be a two by four board i  mean can you tell me what they're up  against as they're trying to do their  job on a daily basis  that that is the work that they do these  highly trained law enforcement officers  are prepared to face those kinds of  risks um but yeah you can see the the  ruthlessness of smugglers and people who  are trying to to evade arrest they're  willing to assault an agent to come into  the country and so they need to be  prepared we need to support them and we  need we need to make sure that they have  the tools that they have that they need  to do their jobs and in a case like this  you would hope that the u.s attorney's  office in san diego will prosecute that  smuggler for the assault on that agent  and so that's that's an important  aspect of this right we want to close  the loop and make sure people are held  to account for the lawlessness that that  could occur on the border absolutely and  real quick we have a picture that we can  pull up of that border patrol agent's  injuries and you know this is just one  example i'm sure many some that we don't  even hear of  right ron a few seconds left  yeah  one of the most assaulted workforces  in law enforcement in the united states  and so yeah that it's unfortunate that  that happened and and i hope that agent  recovers quickly uh and is back on the  job soon okay ron vitello retired acting  ice director thank you so much for  waking up early and joining us  appreciate your time  have a good time

